[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"SRP: Theory, Identification of Responsibilities, and Separation of Concerns"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to the Single Responsibility Principle"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Single Responsibility Principle (SRP) is the first of the five SOLID principles of object-oriented design, coined by Robert C. Martin. SRP states that a class should have one, and only one, reason to change. This means a class should only be responsible for a single part of the functionality provided by the software, and that responsibility should be entirely encapsulated by the class."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding 'Responsibilities'"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the context of SRP, a 'responsibility' is defined as a reason to change. When requirements change, that change will be manifested through a change in responsibility. If a class takes on multiple responsibilities, it has multiple avenues for change. Consequently, modifications to one responsibility can inadvertently break the behavior of another responsibility."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"A responsibility represents a functional domain or actor within the system."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Different actors or stakeholders request changes for different reasons."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Coupling different responsibilities leads to fragile architectures and difficult maintenance cycles."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Anti-Pattern: The God Class"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"A common violation of SRP is the 'God Class' or 'Fat Class'. This occurs when a single class handles business logic, database operations, logging, and data formatting all at once. Consider the following violation example in C#:"},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"public class UserReportManager {
    public string Username { get; set; }

    public void GenerateReport() {
        // Business logic for report generation
    }

    public void SaveToDatabase() {
        // Database logic
    }

    public void LogError(string message) {
        // Logging logic
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, UserReportManager is responsible for reporting logic, persistence, and logging. If the database schema changes, the logging mechanism changes, or the report formatting changes, this class must be modified."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Refactoring for Separation of Concerns"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"To adhere to SRP, we must separate these concerns into distinct, focused classes. Each class should address a single operational boundary."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"public class UserReport {
    public string Username { get; set; }
    public string GenerateReport() {
        return "Report for " + Username;
    }
}

public class UserRepository {
    public void Save(UserReport report) {
        // Handles only database persistence
    }
}

public class Logger {
    public void Log(string message) {
        // Handles only error and event logging
    }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Benefits of Implementing SRP"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Improved Maintainability: Smaller classes are easier to read, understand, and modify."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Enhanced Testability: Classes with single responsibilities require fewer mocks and setup routines in unit tests."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reduced Coupling: Changes in one part of the application have minimal ripple effects on other modules."},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Single Responsibility Principle is foundational to writing robust, scalable software. By carefully identifying responsibilities and enforcing separation of concerns, developers can build modular systems that gracefully adapt to evolving business requirements."}]
